## Why Asked
This question evaluates understanding of fundamental sorting algorithms and their practical trade-offs in real-world applications.

## Key Concepts
- Divide and conquer strategies
- Partitioning vs merging approaches
- In-place vs auxiliary space requirements
- Algorithm stability and performance characteristics

## Code Example
```
// Quicksort partition
function partition(arr, low, high) {
  const pivot = arr[high];
  let i = low - 1;
  for (let j = low; j < high; j++) {
    if (arr[j] < pivot) {
      i++;
      [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
    }
  }
  [arr[i + 1], arr[high]] = [arr[high], arr[i + 1]];
  return i + 1;
}

// Mergesort merge
function merge(left, right) {
  const result = [];
  let i = 0, j = 0;

  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i] <= right[j]) {
      result.push(left[i++]);
    } else {
      result.push(right[j++]);
    }
  }

  return result.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend topological ordering with DP to compute the longest-path in a weighted DAG and count the number of critical paths, including multiple sources and modulo arithmetic.

## Key Concepts
- Longest-path in DAG with node durations and edge setup weights
- Topological sort (Kahn) to process in dependency order
- DP for counting number of longest paths with modulo

## Code Example
```javascript
function countCriticalPaths(N, edges, dur) {
  const MOD = 1000000007;
  const incoming = Array.from({length: N}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(N).fill(0);

  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    incoming[v].push([u, w]);
    indeg[v]++;
  }

  const EFT = dur.slice();
  const count = Array(N).fill(0);
  const q = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (indeg[i] === 0) {
      count[i] = 1;
      q.push(i);
    }
  }

  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift();
    for (const [v, w] of incoming[u]) {
      const newEFT = EFT[u] + w + dur[v];
      if (newEFT > EFT[v]) {
        EFT[v] = newEFT;
        count[v] = count[u];
      } else if (newEFT === EFT[v]) {
        count[v] = (count[v] + count[u]) % MOD;
      }
      indeg[u]--;
      if (indeg[u] === 0) q.push(u);
    }
  }

  let maxEFT = 0, totalCritical = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) {
    if (EFT[i] > maxEFT) {
      maxEFT = EFT[i];
      totalCritical = count[i];
    } else if (EFT[i] === maxEFT) {
      totalCritical = (totalCritical + count[i]) % MOD;
    }
  }
  return { EFT, maxEFT, criticalPaths: totalCritical };
}
```

## Complexity
- Time: O(N + M) for topological sort and DP
- Space: O(N + M) for adjacency and arrays

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply DFS for topological ordering and then DP-style relaxation on a DAG to compute shortest paths without a heap. Also checks path reconstruction via a parent array.

## Key Concepts

- DFS-based topological sort
- DAG single-source shortest path via relaxation in topo order
- Path reconstruction with a parent array
- Complexity: O(n+m)

## Code Example

```javascript
function shortestPathDAG(n, edges, start, target) {
  const g = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) g[u].push([v, w]);
  const visited = Array(n).fill(false);
  const topo = [];
  function dfs(u){
    visited[u] = true;
    for (const [v] of g[u]) if (!visited[v]) dfs(v);
    topo.push(u);
  }
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (!visited[i]) dfs(i);
  topo.reverse();
  const INF = Number.POSITIVE_INFINITY;
  const dist = Array(n).fill(INF);
  const parent = Array(n).fill(-1);
  dist[start] = 0;
  for (const u of topo) {
    if (dist[u] === INF) continue;
    for (const [v, w] of g[u]) {
      if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
        dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
        parent[v] = u;
      }
    }
  }
  if (dist[target] === INF) return {time: -1, path: []};
  const path = [];
  for (let x = target; x != -1; x = parent[x]) path.push(x);
  path.reverse();
  return {time: dist[target], path};
}
```

